.TH SOKOBAN 6
.SH NAME
sokoban, sok - push boxes onto goals
.SH SYNOPSIS
sokoban [-l level] [level-file]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Move the player (@) and push every box ($) onto a goal (.). A box on a goal
is shown as *, and a player standing on a goal as +. Boxes cannot be pulled.
.PP
The supplied collection contains 107 SokEvo puzzles by Lee J Haywood.
His attribution and distribution terms are installed in /usr/lib/sok/README.
.SH CONTROLS
Arrow keys, hjkl or WASD move. U undoes up to 64 moves. R asks to restart the
current puzzle. Q asks to quit. Ctrl-R redraws the screen. Ctrl-C quits and
restores the terminal; Ctrl-D or input EOF also exits.
.PP
The terminal must have at least 32 columns and 18 rows. The game uses ANSI
cursor controls and restores the main screen on exit.
.SH PROGRESS
Completing a default puzzle saves the next level in $HOME/.sokoban. Existing
progress is loaded on the next launch. An explicit level or custom level file
does not change saved progress. Use shutdown to persist the guest filesystem.
.SH OPTIONS
-l selects a level numbered from 1. --help displays usage.
.SH FILE FORMAT
Custom files use native C16 characters, not packed host octets. Each level
record occupies 512 characters: 448 row-major map cells (14 by 32), player row,
player column, boxes on goals, total boxes, then padding. Record zero is reserved
for the collection title. Playable records are checked for bounds, tiles and
matching box/goal counts. Out-of-map moves are rejected.
.SH FILES
/usr/lib/sok/sok.levels - supplied puzzles
.PP
$HOME/.sokoban - next default puzzle, stored as decimal text
